The volume of the first cone is $\blueD{\dfrac{\pi r^2 h}{3}}$.

Let's take $\dfrac{\pi \goldD r^2 h}{3}$ and replace $\goldD r$ with $\goldD{2r}$:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{\pi (\goldD{2r})^2 h}{3}&=\dfrac{\pi 2^2r^2 h}{3}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{4\cdot \pi r^2 h}{3}
\\\\
&=4\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi r^2 h}{3}}\right)
\end{align}$

We started with an expression where $r$ is increased by a factor of $2$ and ended with the overall expression increased by a factor of $4$.
